*Abstract.* A panel can conceal a remembered object and its empty location equally well. We audit a robot's decision to record removal by rendering both states from the same camera pose. The audit comprises 1,280 two-image queries from 16 layouts shared by Isaac Sim and MuJoCo. After a 6 cm camera shift and 3° yaw with the blocker parked, dense registration increased correct removal decisions over homography from 67/80 to 76/80 in Isaac Sim and from 63/80 to 78/80 in MuJoCo. It introduced two false deletions on MuJoCo shelves and failed the prespecified improvement criterion. At 88 fully hidden positions, MuJoCo produced identical inputs across opposite states. Homography abstained on all 88 pairs. Dense geometry declared removal in both states on one. A retrospective comparison on the same images tested the authors' trained STILL verifier against zero-shot Qwen3.5-9B. STILL missed 178 of 640 removals and falsely deleted 36 of 640 retained objects, compared with 221 misses and 51 false deletions for Qwen. STILL deleted both states on 11 identical pairs. These pairs identify updates for which even a physically correct removal decision lacks distinguishing evidence.
